<% use Mojo::JSON qw/decode_json/; %>
<% no warnings 'uninitialized'; %>
<%
	# Every AI model choice and provider credential in one place.
	my @roles = (
		[ 'help_assistant',          'Help guide',           'chat' ],
		[ 'eval_assistant',          'Evaluation',           'chat' ],
		[ 'visual_assistant',        'Vision (reads images)', 'vision' ],
		[ 'text_to_image_assistant', 'Image generation',     'image' ],
		[ 'chatbot_model',           'Pen chat (default)',   'chat' ],
		[ 'coding_assistant',        'Coding (self-healing)', 'chat' ],
	);
	my $role_settings = &subs::settings_grabber({ app => 'mail', settings => [ map { $_->[0] } @roles ] });
	my $catalogue = &subs::ai_model_catalogue();
	my $providers = &subs::ai_providers();
	my $key_values = &subs::settings_grabber({ app => 'mail', settings => [ &subs::ai_key_settings() ] });
	# which providers can answer here at all: a key entered, or a running Ollama.
	# The role lists below only offer these - the rest are named under them, so it
	# is clear which of them wants a key (or a daemon). Until the tree restarts
	# onto this build the helper does not exist yet, and the panel keeps its old
	# shape for those requests rather than dying on it.
	my %provider_ready = map { $_->{'id'} => (defined &subs::ai_provider_ready ? &subs::ai_provider_ready($_->{'id'}) : 1) } @{$catalogue};
	# which billing buckets are stood down (out of credit) - one check per bucket
	my %bucket_ok;
	foreach my $p ( @{$catalogue} ) {
		foreach my $m ( @{ $p->{'models'} } ) {
			$bucket_ok{ $m->{'bucket'} } = &subs::ai_bucket_available($m->{'bucket'}) unless exists $bucket_ok{ $m->{'bucket'} };
		}
	}
	my @down = sort grep { !$bucket_ok{$_} && $_ ne 'ollama' && $_ ne 'cloudflare' } keys %bucket_ok;
%>
<div class="cfg">
	<h2>Platform</h2>
	<p class="cfg-note">Every AI model choice and provider key, in one place.</p>

	<h3>Agent models</h3>
	<p class="cfg-note">Which model each assistant uses. Each list offers only models that fit the role, and marks whether a model is free or paid.</p>
	<% if (@down) { %>
		<p class="cfg-note">Out of credit just now, so their paid models are hidden:
			<% foreach my $b ( @down ) { %><span style="white-space:nowrap;"><b><%= $b %></b> <button class="ai_avail_clear" bucket="<%= $b %>" style="font-size:11px;">Clear</button></span> <% } %>
			They come back on their own after a while, or once the account is topped up.</p>
	<% } %>
	<div class="cfg-card">
		<% foreach my $r ( @roles ) {
			my ($setting, $label, $cap) = @{$r};
			my $current = $role_settings->{$setting} // '';
			my $shown = 0;
		%>
			<div class="cfg-row">
				<label><%= $label %></label>
				<select class="configure_input" app="mail" setting="<%= $setting %>">
					<option value="">— none —</option>
					<% foreach my $provider ( @{$catalogue} ) {
						next unless $provider_ready{ $provider->{'id'} };
						my @models = grep {
							my $m = $_;
							scalar( grep { $_ eq $cap } @{ $m->{'caps'} } ) && (!$m->{'paid'} || $bucket_ok{ $m->{'bucket'} })
						} @{ $provider->{'models'} };
						next unless scalar @models;
					%>
						<optgroup label="<%= $provider->{'label'} %>">
							<% foreach my $m ( @models ) {
								my @mcaps = grep { $_ ne 'other' } @{ $m->{'caps'} };
							%>
								<option value="<%= $m->{'value'} %>" <% if ($current eq $m->{'value'}) { %>selected="selected"<% $shown = 1; } %>><%= $m->{'name'} %> · <%= join('+', @mcaps) %> · <%= $m->{'paid'} ? 'paid' : 'free' %></option>
							<% } %>
						</optgroup>
					<% } %>
					<%# a model already chosen that does not fit, or is paid and stood down, is kept so it is not silently dropped %>
					<% if ($current && !$shown) { %>
						<option value="<%= $current %>" selected="selected"><%= $current %> (kept — not offered here just now)</option>
					<% } %>
				</select>
			</div>
		<% } %>
	</div>

	<% my @not_ready = grep { !$provider_ready{ $_->{'id'} } } @{$catalogue}; %>
	<% if (@not_ready) { %>
		<p class="cfg-note">Not offered until they can answer: <% foreach my $p (@not_ready) { %><b><%= $p->{'label'} %></b><%= ($p->{'id'} eq 'ollama' ? ' (not running)' : ' (no key)') %><%= ($p->{'id'} eq $not_ready[-1]->{'id'} ? '' : ', ') %><% } %>. Paste a key under Provider access and press Plat again, or start Ollama for the local models.</p>
	<% } %>

	<h3>Self-healing</h3>
	<p class="cfg-note">With a Coding model set, the tree's own error output is watched (the pty log and manager.log). Each new error appears in the IDE's coding panel with a fix the Coding agent proposes; nothing is written until you press Apply there. Tick Apply automatically and each proposal is written, checked (perl -c, with a rollback if it fails) and shipped at once - a file with uncommitted work stands down and waits for you, and the day's token budget still applies. Tick Watch the Guide and the Guide's own saved conversations are read the same way: a turn that went nowhere - a thing not found, a command that could not run, a closing line that says so - is staged for the Coding agent to adjust the Guide's training itself (its rules and verbs). Fixed so far, under the tick, is the record: press review to read every error that has been fixed and shipped.</p>
	<div class="cfg-card">
		<div class="cfg-row">
			<label>Watch errors</label>
			<% my $watching = (&subs::setting_grabber({ app => 'mail', setting => 'error_watch' }) // ''); %>
			<input type="checkbox" class="configure_input" app="mail" setting="error_watch" <% if ($watching eq 'on') { %>checked<% } %>>
		</div>
		<div class="cfg-row">
			<label>Watch the Guide</label>
			<% my $guide_watching = (&subs::setting_grabber({ app => 'mail', setting => 'guide_watch' }) // ''); %>
			<input type="checkbox" class="configure_input" app="mail" setting="guide_watch" <% if ($guide_watching eq 'on') { %>checked<% } %>>
		</div>
		<div class="cfg-row">
			<label>Apply automatically</label>
			<% my $autoing = (&subs::setting_grabber({ app => 'mail', setting => 'error_watch_auto' }) // ''); %>
			<input type="checkbox" class="configure_input" app="mail" setting="error_watch_auto" <% if ($autoing eq 'on') { %>checked<% } %>>
		</div>
		<div class="cfg-row">
			<label>Fixed so far</label>
			<%
				my $fixed_n = 0;
				eval {
					my $fixed_row = &subs::db_query('select count(*) as n from staged_fixes where status = ?', 'applied')->hashes->[0];
					$fixed_n = ($fixed_row->{'n'} || 0) if $fixed_row;
				};
			%>
			<span class="cfg-inline">
				<span id="coding_fixed_open" class="hover" hint="Review the errors the Coding agent has fixed, committed and pushed" style="cursor:pointer;text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:2px;">review</span>
				<span class="cfg-note" style="margin:0;"><%= $fixed_n ? "$fixed_n fixed" : 'nothing yet' %></span>
			</span>
		</div>
		<div id="coding_fixed_list" style="display:none;margin:2px 0 8px;padding:6px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--line);max-height:320px;overflow:auto;font-size:12px;line-height:1.35;"></div>
		<div class="cfg-row">
			<label>Tokens/day</label>
			<% my $watch_budget = (&subs::setting_grabber({ app => 'mail', setting => 'error_watch_budget' }) // ''); %>
			<input type="text" class="configure_input" app="mail" setting="error_watch_budget" value="<%= $watch_budget %>" placeholder="1000000 — 0 for no cap">
		</div>
	</div>

	<h3>Provider access</h3>
	<p class="cfg-note">Keys and accounts for each provider. Secrets are stored encrypted: a key that is already set shows “••• set” — leave it alone to keep it, or type a new value to replace it.</p>
	<% foreach my $p ( @{$providers} ) { %>
		<div class="cfg-card">
			<h4 style="margin-top:0;"><%= $p->{'label'} %></h4>
			<% foreach my $k ( @{ $p->{'keys'} } ) { %>
				<% my ($name, $label) = @{$k}; my $val = $key_values->{$name}; my $is_secret = ($name =~ /_(key|token)$/) ? 1 : 0; my $set = (defined $val && length $val) ? 1 : 0; %>
				<div class="cfg-row">
					<label><%= $label %></label>
					<% if ($is_secret) { %>
						<span class="cfg-inline">
							<input type="password" class="configure_input" app="mail" setting="<%= $name %>" value=""
								placeholder="<%= $set ? '••• set — type to replace' : 'not set' %>"
								data-set="<%= $set %>">
							<% if ($set) { %><span class="cfg-note" style="margin:0;white-space:nowrap;">✔ set</span><% } %>
						</span>
					<% } else { %>
						<input type="text" class="configure_input" app="mail" setting="<%= $name %>" value="<%= $val %>">
					<% } %>
				</div>
			<% } %>
		</div>
	<% } %>
	<p class="cfg-note">Ollama and Cloudflare model lists are read from each service when this panel opens; the other providers' lists are built in. Models are addressed as <code>provider:model</code> — a bare name goes to Ollama, and <code>@cf/…</code> to Cloudflare. The lists only offer providers that can answer: a key entered (and an account not out of credit), or a local Ollama that is running.</p>

	<h3>Guide commands (runtime)</h3>
	<p class="cfg-note">Extra verbs the Guide can run, added without a code change. Each entry is JSON: a <code>name</code>, a <code>desc</code>, an optional <code>args</code> map, and a <code>primitive</code> with its <code>params</code>. The primitives are <code>say</code>, <code>open_app</code>, <code>set_setting</code>, <code>run_command</code>, <code>toggle</code>, <code>stage</code>, <code>find</code> and <code>play_music</code>; a param value may contain <code>{name}</code> to take one of the model's arguments. The list is a setting, so it syncs to every machine and they all learn the verb.</p>
	<textarea id="guide_verbs_json" spellcheck="false" style="width:100%;box-sizing:border-box;min-height:140px;font-family:monospace;font-size:12px;border-radius:6px;border:1px solid var(--line);background:var(--panel);color:var(--ink);padding:8px;"><%= &subs::setting_grabber({ app => '__president', setting => 'guide_verbs', device => 'all' }) || '[]' %></textarea>
	<div class="cfg-row">
		<button id="guide_verbs_save" class="button" style="padding:6px 12px;border-radius:8px;border:2px solid var(--line);cursor:pointer;font-weight:bold;">Save commands</button>
		<span id="guide_verbs_status" class="cfg-note" style="margin:0;"></span>
	</div>
</div>
